Imports:
  - Types:
      - Backend
    From: server/storage/backend
  - Types:
      - Lessor
    From: server/lease
  - Types:
      - KV
    From: server/storage/mvcc
  - Types:
      - AuthStore
    From: server/auth
  - Types:
      - RaftCluster
    From: server/etcdserver/api/membership

Usages:
  decorator_chain: |
    The base execution logic is wrapped by independently composable decorators, applied
    outermost-to-innermost as: corruption lockout (active only during a CORRUPT alarm), space-
    cap rejection (active only during a NOSPACE alarm), RBAC authorization, backend byte-size
    quota accounting, then the base applier that actually touches storage/lease/auth. Each
    concern can be added, removed, or reordered independently of the others because every layer
    implements the same interface and only ever delegates to the layer it wraps. Add a new
    cross-cutting concern (e.g. rate limiting, audit logging) as a new decorator in this chain
    rather than modifying the base applier.

Annotations: |
  This cell is the raft-committed-entry execution layer sitting between raft consensus and the
  storage/lease/auth cells: once one raft log entry commits, this cell is the sole place that
  turns it into calls against `KV`, `Lessor`, `AuthStore`, and `RaftCluster`. It is structured as
  a Chain-of-Responsibility of decorators per `decorator_chain` — this is etcd's real, existing
  extension point for adding a new cross-cutting behavior to every mutating/reading request
  uniformly.

  Use `Backend` only for byte-size quota accounting, never to bypass `KV`/`Lessor`/`AuthStore`
  for the actual data operations.

---

"UberApplier()":
  location: uber_applier.go
  annotations: |
    Owns the full decorator chain described in `decorator_chain` and rebuilds its alarm-
    dependent outer layers whenever a NOSPACE or CORRUPT alarm is activated or cleared.
  methods:
    "Apply(request InternalRaftRequest) -> result:ApplyResult": |
      Execute one committed raft request against the head of the decorator chain.

      Algorithm:
      1. Run the chain's apply hook top-to-bottom (each decorator may short-circuit, e.g. on
         an active CORRUPT alarm)
      2. Dispatch on the request's concrete type (Put, Range, Txn, LeaseGrant, AuthEnable, ...)
         to the matching typed method, invoked again through the same chain outermost-first
      3. Return the leaf result (or the first short-circuiting decorator's rejection)

"NewUberApplier(options ApplierOptions) -> applier:UberApplier":
  location: uber_applier.go
  annotations: |
    Build the static inner chain (authorization wrapping quota wrapping the base applier) once,
    from `options`, and compute the alarm-dependent outer layers for the alarms active at
    construction time.

    `options`: injected handles to every cell this layer executes requests against.

"ApplierOptions()":
  location: interface.go
  annotations: |
    The dependency-injection bundle handed to `NewUberApplier`; one field per cell this layer
    is capable of mutating on behalf of a committed raft request.
  properties:
    "KV -> KV": |
      The imported `KV` that Put/Range/DeleteRange/Txn/Compaction requests are executed
      against.
    "Lessor -> Lessor": |
      The imported `Lessor` that LeaseGrant/LeaseRevoke/LeaseCheckpoint requests are executed
      against.
    "AuthStore -> AuthStore": |
      The imported `AuthStore` that Auth*/User*/Role* requests are executed against, and that
      the authorization decorator consults before every data-plane request.
    "Cluster -> RaftCluster": |
      The imported `RaftCluster` that cluster-version and member-attribute requests mutate.
    "Backend -> Backend": |
      The imported `Backend` the quota decorator inspects to decide whether a write would
      exceed the configured byte-size quota.
    "QuotaBackendBytesCfg -> int64": |
      The configured backend byte-size quota threshold.

---

Author: Goga
CreatedAt: 26/08/26
Description: |
  The raft-committed-entry execution layer: a decorator chain (corruption lockout, space cap,
  authorization, quota, base execution) that is etcd's real extension point for adding new
  cross-cutting request behavior uniformly across Put/Range/Txn/lease/auth operations.
